{{also|Upland}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|upland}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ūpland|t=the country}}; equivalent to {{pre|en|up|land}}.

The word as an adjective developed in Middle English.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈʌplənd/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-upland.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈʌplənd/|/ˈəp-/|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌplənd|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|up|land}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|interior of country with generally higher elevation}} The [[area]] in the [[interior]] of a [[country]] with a [[generally]] [[higher]] [[elevation]].
#: {{ant|en|lowland#Noun}}
#: {{hyper|en|land#Noun|;|area#Noun|;|region#Noun}}
#: {{cot|en|inland#Noun|midland#Noun|outland#Noun}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|highland<id:Q878223>|upcountry#Noun}}
# {{senseid|en|the country, rural area, as opposed to the town or city}} {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[country#English:_the country, rural area, as opposed to the town or city|country]], as contrasted with the [[town]].
#: {{ant|en|city#Noun|town#Noun|conurbation#Noun}}

